  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several electrochemical optical methods used to study molecules at electrode surfaces. A simple energy sketch at the top left indicates an interface with the Fermi level, setting the electrochemical context. The other panels show a liquid electrochemical cell with working, counter, and reference electrodes, combined with different light-based readouts.\n\nAcross the figure, nanoparticles or metal films at the bottom of the cell act as plasmonic or reflective surfaces that enhance signals from adsorbed molecules. One scheme uses dark-field white-light scattering to probe particles in solution. Others use focused laser excitation for Raman-based measurements, including EC-SERS and EC-SHINERS, where shell-isolated nanoparticles appear to boost Raman signals near the surface. A tip-based arrangement illustrates EC-TERS, where a sharp probe concentrates the field for nanoscale spectroscopy. Additional panels show nonlinear and infrared-sensitive approaches, including EC-SFG and EC-SEIRA, using prism- or window-based optical coupling to detect interfacial vibrational signals. A scanning electrochemical setup with a capillary appears to map local activity on a single particle.\n\nOverall, the image compares complementary ways to monitor electrochemical interfaces, surface adsorbates, and local reaction environments with enhanced optical sensitivity.",
